//! Entries slice — Batch 207 (Plan 207) — the 10-07 staged leads: two
//! `tracel-ai/cubecl` commits (MIT OR Apache-2.0; ephemeral clone
//! `.raw/b207-cubecl`, removed at close). Quotes re-verified VERBATIM at
//! the pins (line numbers from `git show <sha>:<path>` at the post-commit
//! files, not hunk-derived).
//!
//! Pack composition (category split B +1, D +1; corpus 771 → 773):
//! - B +1 `minifloat-normal-code-shift-bias-diff-decode` (#1777 — the
//!   one-shift+bias-diff normal decode, the placed E2M1 decode, and the
//!   `emulated_conversion` admission predicate, all one commit)
//! - D +1 `profile-window-drains-queued-work-before-open` (#1751 — the
//!   measurement-honesty rule: a profile window opened behind unrelated
//!   queued work times that work as its own and the tuner elects outliers)
//!
//! DEDUP (run at authoring, both vocabularies): zero prior owners — the
//! decode-mechanism family owns replicated-LUT gather (B194), parity +
//! field-arithmetic tables, and ue8m0 scale ENCODING; no
//! profile-window/timestamp/drain rule existed; the only autotune rule is
//! `hot-swap-weights-preserve-autotune-caches` (runtime caches, different
//! axis).

use super::KernelRule;

#[allow(clippy::too_many_lines)]
pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── B: the minifloat decode (#1777) — the fused one-shift+
        // bias-diff normal decode, the placed E2M1 f16 decode (the lift
        // folded into the caller's block scale), and the emulated-
        // conversion admission predicate, all one commit.
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "minifloat-normal-code-shift-bias-diff-decode".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (the minifloat normal decode: one shift + one bias-difference add, select on magnitude, sign once): an fp8/e2m1 code's MAGNITUDE — its exponent and mantissa bits together — is already an `f32`'s exponent and mantissa in order, so a normal code decodes as ONE shift of the magnitude into mantissa-bit position plus ONE add of the bias difference pre-shifted into the mantissa field, where the decode took the two fields apart and put them back. THE SOURCE LAW, verbatim (commit message): 'A code's magnitude is already an f32's exponent and mantissa in order, so a normal code is one shift and one add of the bias difference on the exponent field, where the decode took the two fields apart and put them back. The subnormal arm is chosen by the magnitude alone, and the sign is one or at the end. Every code decodes as before: the exhaustive decode tests pass for e4m3 and e5m2 on Metal, and fail when the bias is off by one.' In-source (minifloat.rs L34-40): 'A code's magnitude, its exponent and mantissa together, is already an `f32`'s exponent and mantissa in order, so a normal code is one shift and one add of the bias difference on the exponent field, rather than the two fields taken apart and put back. A zero exponent leaves the magnitude as the mantissa alone, a whole number of the format's smallest step, which is the one place the layouts disagree and the one select the values owe. The sign rides as its bit. Every code decodes exactly, and only through `u32` and `f32`.' THE THREE LINES, verbatim (minifloat.rs): 'let magnitude = bits & Vector::new(FP8_MAGNITUDE_MASK);' then 'let normal = (magnitude << Vector::new(mantissa_shift)) + Vector::new(rebias);' with rebias = (F32_EXPONENT_BIAS - format.bias()) << F32_MANTISSA_BITS — the bias difference PRE-SHIFTED into the mantissa field, so one add lands the exponent rebase and the f32 mantissa position together — then 'let subnormal = Vector::<u32, N>::reinterpret(Vector::<f32, N>::cast_from(magnitude) * Vector::new(subnormal_step));'; the select predicate is 'magnitude.less_than(&Vector::new(smallest_normal))' (smallest_normal = 1<<mantissa_bits) — MAGNITUDE alone, never the exponent field; the specials select compares magnitude.equal/greater_equal(special) with special = exponent_mask << mantissa_bits; and the sign is applied ONCE at the end: 'Vector::<f32, N>::reinterpret(result | sign)'. THE CARRY-SAFETY CLAUSE, verbatim (minifloat.rs L57-58 comment): 'The add cannot carry out of the exponent field: the largest exponent, rebiased, still fits it.' — the safety property that makes the fused form exact. MEASURED CONTEXT (commit message, SOURCE-REPORTED, never a bar): 'Measured in an NVFP4 gemv on an M2 Pro, where one ue4m3 scale is decoded per sixteen values: about 3% of the kernel's time.' THE PLACED-DECODE ARM (fp4.rs): 'pub const E2M1_F16_LIFT: f32 = 16384.0;' (L161, renamed from F16_LIFT and made pub), and e2m1_words_to_f16 is now just 'e2m1_words_to_f16_placed::<W, V>(words) * Vector::new(f16::new(E2M1_F16_LIFT))' (L183); the placed fn's doc, verbatim (fp4.rs L186-188): 'The eight codes of each word of `words`, lowest nibble first, placed as `f16` each [`E2M1_F16_LIFT`] short of its value, exactly: what a caller that multiplies the values by a factor anyway, a block scale, takes, multiplying the lift into that factor once rather than paying a multiply a value.' (commit message: 'e2m1_words_to_f16_placed is the f16 pair decode without its lift: each value placed 2^14 (E2M1_F16_LIFT) short of itself, exactly, for a caller that multiplies by a block scale anyway and folds the lift into it rather than paying a multiply a value. e2m1_words_to_f16 is it lifted.') THE ADMISSION ARM (features.rs L59-63, verbatim): 'Types whose [`Conversion`](TypeUsage::Conversion) the compiler emulates in software, from their bits, rather than with an instruction of the device. A kernel that decodes such a type from its bits itself pays no more than the conversion would, and may move what the emulation spends into work it already does.' — `Features::conversion_is_emulated` (features.rs L249-251) + `DeviceProperties::register_emulated_conversion` (properties.rs L514); the Metal backend calls 'props.register_emulated_conversion(ElemType::Float(ty));' (metal.rs L277) for its fp8 and fp4. DECISION CONTENT: (1) the old decode did mask-extract exponent + mask-extract mantissa, rebiased and re-shifted the two fields apart, plus a separate subnormal path selected on exponent==0 and the sign OR'd into BOTH arms — the new decode treats the magnitude bits as ALREADY an f32's exponent-then-mantissa in order, so the whole normal path is one shift plus one add of the pre-shifted bias difference; (2) the subnormal select is chosen by MAGNITUDE alone ('< smallest_normal'): zero exponent means the magnitude IS the mantissa alone, a whole number of the format's smallest step — the one place the layouts disagree, hence the one select the values owe; (3) the sign is ONE or at the end, not per-arm; (4) the add cannot carry out of the exponent field (largest exponent, rebiased, still fits) — the safety clause that makes the fused form exact, and the exhaustive-decode + off-by-one-bias tests are its instrument; (5) the PLACED arm: decode WITHOUT the constant lift when the caller multiplies by a block scale anyway — fold the 2^14 lift into the caller's scale factor ONCE instead of paying a multiply per value; the lifted fn IS the placed fn times the lift; (6) the ADMISSION predicate: when the backend emulates the type's conversion in software (Metal registers fp8/fp4), decode-from-bits in-kernel costs no more than the conversion would — the admission that licenses spending kernel ALU on the decode and folding what the emulation spends into work the kernel already does. ANTI-REGIME: a native convert instruction exists on the device (use it — `conversion_is_emulated` false IS the refusal); formats whose layouts disagree beyond the subnormal boundary (the one-select property is specific to exponent-at-bottom layouts); callers that consume decoded values WITHOUT a per-block multiply gain nothing from the placed arm (keep the lifted fn). Distinct from fp4-nibble-gather-lut-replicated-index-width (B194 — the replicated-LUT GATHER decode, code→code conversion; THIS is code→VALUE arithmetic with no table; its Batch-199 rider adds the parity-nibble + field-arithmetic + exact-bitcast instance — the decode-mechanism family's members are gather-LUT, field arithmetic, and now bit-PLACEMENT + offset-folding as the third), from quantized-stored-bytes-not-widened-patterns (B199 — the storage-form axis: hold the wire bytes; THIS is the in-kernel decode arithmetic ON those bytes — compose, never merge), from enumerable-domain-bit-identity-admission-gate (B195 — the exhaustive-enumeration admission this decode's exhaustive e4m3/e5m2 tests + off-by-one-bias guard instantiate), from dtype-native-source-read-with-shared-admission-predicate (B176 — licenses EXACT widening into a compute dtype under a shared admission; THIS lands a new ADMISSION-PREDICATE MEMBER beside it, the emulated-conversion predicate — composing, not merging), from ue8m0-pow2-scale-as-biased-exponent-byte (B111 — scale ENCODING, not decode placement), and from bit-exact-dispatch-fusion-boundary-emulation (B97 — the fusion-boundary emulation contract, not conversion admission). Source: tracel-ai/cubecl @ 9f6bb84fb8da51cae518f756e6adb598de949dde (#1777) -- crates/cubecl-core/src/post_processing/minifloat.rs L34-40,44-66,88 (the doc + the fused decode), crates/cubecl-core/src/post_processing/fp4.rs L161,183,186-194 (E2M1_F16_LIFT + the placed decode), crates/cubecl-ir/src/features.rs L59-63,249-251 + crates/cubecl-ir/src/properties.rs L514 + crates/cubecl-wgpu/src/backend/metal.rs L277 (the emulated-conversion admission), MIT OR Apache-2.0. NotBenchable here (the ~3% is an M2 Pro source-reported figure — provenance, never a bar; the exhaustive-decode + off-by-one-bias tests are the exactness instrument). Batch 207 / Plan 207.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: the fp8 code is SPLIT into exponent + mantissa fields, each\n// rebiased and re-shifted separately — two field round-trips — with a\n// SECOND subnormal path keyed on exponent==0 and the sign OR'd into\n// BOTH arms; the block-scale caller then pays a multiply PER VALUE.\nconst EXP_MASK: u8 = 0x78; // e4m3: the 4 exponent bits of the low 7\nconst MAN_MASK: u8 = 0x07; // e4m3: the 3 mantissa bits\nconst FMT_BIAS: i32 = 7;\nconst F32_BIAS: i32 = 127;\nconst MAN_BITS: u32 = 3;\n\nfn decode_fp8_naive(bits: u8) -> f32 {\n    let sign = u32::from(bits & 0x80) << 24; // applied per arm, twice\n    let exp = i32::from((bits & EXP_MASK) >> MAN_BITS); // field apart 1\n    let man = u32::from(bits & MAN_MASK); // field apart 2\n    if exp == 0 {\n        // subnormal arm: a separate path, keyed on the EXPONENT field\n        let step = 2.0f32.powi(FMT_BIAS - F32_BIAS - MAN_BITS as i32 + 1);\n        f32::from_bits(sign) * f32::from(man) * step // sign OR'd here\n    } else {\n        // normal arm: rebase the exponent, reassemble the two fields\n        let rebased = (exp - FMT_BIAS + F32_BIAS) as u32;\n        let rebuilt = (rebased << MAN_BITS) | man;\n        f32::from_bits(sign) * f32::from_bits(rebuilt) // ...and here\n    }\n}\n\n// the block-scale caller on the naive path: a scale multiply PER VALUE —\n// the waste the placed arm folds into the factor ONCE\nfn scale_block_naive(codes: &[u8], block_scale: f32) -> Vec<f32> {\n    codes.iter().map(|&b| decode_fp8_naive(b) * block_scale).collect()\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the fused decode — the code's MAGNITUDE (exponent +\n// mantissa together) is already an f32's exponent-then-mantissa in\n// order, so the normal path is ONE shift + ONE add of the bias\n// difference PRE-SHIFTED into the mantissa field; the subnormal select\n// keys on the MAGNITUDE alone; the sign is ONE or at the end. Exact by\n// the carry clause: the add cannot leave the exponent field.\nconst FP8_MAGNITUDE_MASK: u8 = 0x7F; // exponent + mantissa together\nconst MAN_BITS: u32 = 3;\nconst FMT_BIAS: i32 = 7;\nconst F32_EXPONENT_BIAS: i32 = 127;\n\nfn decode_fp8(bits: u8) -> f32 {\n    let sign = u32::from(bits & 0x80) << 24;\n    let magnitude = u32::from(bits & FP8_MAGNITUDE_MASK);\n    let smallest_normal = 1u32 << MAN_BITS;\n    // rebias = (F32_EXPONENT_BIAS - format.bias()) << F32_MANTISSA_BITS:\n    // the bias difference PRE-SHIFTED, so ONE add lands the exponent\n    // rebase and the f32 mantissa position together\n    let rebias: u32 = ((F32_EXPONENT_BIAS - FMT_BIAS) as u32) << MAN_BITS;\n    let normal = (magnitude << MAN_BITS) + rebias;\n    let subnormal_step = 2.0f32.powi(FMT_BIAS - F32_EXPONENT_BIAS - MAN_BITS as i32 + 1);\n    let subnormal = (magnitude as f32 * subnormal_step).to_bits();\n    // select by MAGNITUDE alone: zero exponent = the mantissa alone, a\n    // whole number of the format's smallest step (the one layout seam)\n    let value = if magnitude < smallest_normal { subnormal } else { normal };\n    f32::from_bits(value | sign) // the sign: ONE or, at the end\n}\n\n// the PLACED arm: the e2m1 pair decode WITHOUT its lift — each value\n// placed 2^14 (E2M1_F16_LIFT) short of itself, exactly — for a caller\n// that multiplies by a block scale anyway: fold the lift into the\n// factor ONCE, not a multiply a value. The lifted fn IS the placed fn\n// times the lift.\nconst E2M1_F16_LIFT: f32 = 16384.0;\n\nfn placed_halves(_word: u16) -> [f32; 2] {\n    [0.0, 0.0] // the two f16 lanes, LIFT-short, no lift applied\n}\n\nfn e2m1_words_to_f16_placed(words: &[u16]) -> Vec<f32> {\n    // the eight codes of each word, lowest nibble first, placed as f16\n    // each E2M1_F16_LIFT short of its value, exactly (the source's f16\n    // placement arithmetic; the exhaustive-decode tests pin exactness)\n    words.iter().flat_map(|&w| placed_halves(w)).collect()\n}\n\nfn e2m1_words_to_f16(words: &[u16]) -> Vec<f32> {\n    e2m1_words_to_f16_placed(words)\n        .into_iter()\n        .map(|v| v * E2M1_F16_LIFT) // the lifted fn: placed * the lift\n        .collect()\n}\n\n// the block-scale caller that takes the placed arm: the factor carries\n// the lift, multiplied in ONCE\nfn scale_block_placed(words: &[u16], block_scale: f32) -> Vec<f32> {\n    let factor = block_scale * E2M1_F16_LIFT; // the lift folded in once\n    e2m1_words_to_f16_placed(words)\n        .into_iter()\n        .map(|v| v * factor)\n        .collect()\n}".to_string(),
        },

        // ── D: dispatch/measurement (#1751) — the profile window drains
        // the queue before it opens on stage-boundary backends; the
        // autotuner's cache-warming read no longer lands in the samples.
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "profile-window-drains-queued-work-before-open".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (a profile window drains the queued work before it opens): a GPU profile window is only as honest as the ordering at its first pass — on Metal, a pass's begin timestamp samples when its ENCODER STARTS, and an encoder is ordered after earlier work only through a buffer the two share, so a window opened behind work it shares nothing with starts BESIDE that work and times its tail and its device contention as the window's own. THE SOURCE LAW, verbatim (commit message): 'Metal samples a pass's begin when its encoder starts and orders an encoder after earlier work only through a buffer the two share. A window opened behind work it shares nothing with started beside it and timed its tail and its contention as the window's own. Autotune queues exactly that work, a read past the caches, before every sample: on an M2 Pro a decode gemv's samples read about three times their duration, except where the read happened to finish first, and the tuner elected those outliers.' AND: 'The backend fact is named once, TimestampSampling, with both consequences on it: a resolve waits for completion (as before), and a window drains the queue before it opens (new).' IN-SOURCE (timings.rs): the enum rename `TimestampAvailability` → `TimestampSampling` (L86) with variants `InCommandStream` (L89, doc: 'As commands of the stream. Vulkan writes a pass's begin at the bottom of the pipe, once every command recorded before it has completed.') and `AtStageBoundaries` (L91, 'At the stage boundaries of the pass's encoder (Metal)'); the backend mapping L97-98 (Metal → AtStageBoundaries, else InCommandStream); `resolves_after_completion` L107-108, doc: 'Metal writes the samples when the encoder retires: a resolve that runs earlier, even from a later command buffer, reads zeros.'; and the NEW predicate `drains_before_window` L119-120, doc: 'Metal samples a pass's begin when its encoder starts, and orders an encoder after earlier work only through a buffer the two share. A pass that shares none starts beside whatever is still running, so the window holds that work's tail, and its contention for the device: a sample queued behind a read past the caches, which shares nothing with it by design, was timed at three times its own duration.' THE FIX ARM (stream.rs L525-529): 'Timings::Device(query) if query.sampling().drains_before_window() => { self.flush(stream_id)?; ... if let Err(err) = self.device.poll(wgpu::PollType::wait_indefinitely()) { log::warn!(\"waiting for the work ahead of a profiled window: {err}\"); } }' — flush, then the poll-wait; the arm's comment, verbatim: 'Waits like system timing's sync, for the reason `drains_before_window` gives: work still running when the window's first pass starts is timed as its own.' THE REGRESSION TEST (tests/profile_window.rs L1-7, macOS-only), verbatim: 'A profile window times its own work, not the work queued ahead of it. … Autotune queues a read past the caches before each sample, a launch that shares nothing with the sample by design, and measured the sample beside it: three times its own duration, except where the read happened to finish first.' DECISION CONTENT: (1) the DOUBLE damage — corrupted measurement AND a wrong autotune election derived from it (the tuner elected the outliers that happened to start beside a finished read); (2) the backend fact is named ONCE (`TimestampSampling`) and BOTH consequences hang off it — resolve timing (as before) + drain-before-window (new); (3) the drain is flush + `device.poll(wait_indefinitely)` — the wait is the system-timing sync's analog; (4) admission polarity: only the AtStageBoundaries backend drains; InCommandStream backends (Vulkan — begin written at the bottom of the pipe behind every earlier command) cannot fail this and keep the cheap flush. ANTI-REGIME: never drain on backends whose timestamps sample in command stream (pure overhead); never 'fix' a corrupted autotune table by re-running the tuner without fixing the window (the election stays wrong); a window whose work shares a buffer with the queued work already orders correctly (the shared-buffer edge) — but the drain is the only guard for the by-design-nothing-shared case (autotune's cache-warming read). Distinct from hot-swap-weights-preserve-autotune-caches (B25 — runtime cache amortization across weight swaps, a lifetime axis, not measurement honesty), from fused-serving-stack-measured-negative-ledger (B189 — the measured-negative ANNOTATION row, five rejected levers recorded; THIS is the positive sibling: fix the window, not just record the negative), from construction-identity-row-classes-replace-probe (B193, kernel_opt — sampling probes cannot certify bit-identity; the same honesty family on the CORRECTNESS axis, vs this TIMING axis), from fallthrough-toggle-oracle (rust_perf B186 — the oracle that catches work toggled OFF the timed path; THIS catches work queuing OUTSIDE the timed region), and from seed-warm-the-scored-seam (rust_perf B181 — warm the seam before scoring; complementary: drain before opening and warm before scoring are opposite polarities of the same window-hygiene law). Source: tracel-ai/cubecl @ 04a894b2a9922de56df4b17a07cca335cd4165e1 (#1751) -- crates/cubecl-wgpu/src/compute/timings.rs L86-120 (TimestampSampling + both predicates), crates/cubecl-wgpu/src/compute/stream.rs L519-536 (the drain arm in start_profile), crates/cubecl-wgpu/tests/profile_window.rs L1-7 (the macOS regression test), MIT OR Apache-2.0. NotBenchable here (the ~3x is an M2 Pro source-reported figure on THEIR autotune lane — provenance, never a bar; the win class is measurement honesty, not kernel throughput). Batch 207 / Plan 207.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: start_profile only FLUSHES the host stream — it never drains\n// work already queued on the device — and the autotuner queues a\n// cache-warming read (sharing nothing with the sample) before EVERY\n// sample. On a stage-boundary backend the sample's begin timestamp\n// fires when its encoder STARTS, beside the still-running read: the\n// samples read ~3x their duration, and min() elects the outliers that\n// happened to start beside a finished read.\nstruct Error;\n\n#[derive(Default)]\nstruct Profiler {\n    warm_read_issued: bool,\n}\n\nimpl Profiler {\n    fn flush(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        Ok(()) // orders the HOST's own submissions — nothing on the device\n    }\n\n    fn time_sample(&mut self, work: u64) -> Result<u64, Error> {\n        // the cache-warming read enters the queue FIRST, every sample\n        self.warm_read_issued = true; // shares no buffer with the sample\n        Ok(3 * work) // the read's tail + contention timed as our own\n    }\n}\n\nfn elect_min_naive(prof: &mut Profiler, works: &[u64]) -> usize {\n    let mut best = 0usize;\n    let mut best_us = u64::MAX;\n    for (i, &w) in works.iter().enumerate() {\n        prof.flush().unwrap(); // the ONLY ordering: host-side\n        let us = prof.time_sample(w).unwrap();\n        if us < best_us {\n            best_us = us;\n            best = i; // a corrupted minimum: an outlier wins the election\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the backend fact named ONCE (TimestampSampling), BOTH\n// consequences hanging off it; only the AtStageBoundaries backend\n// drains — InCommandStream backends (the begin written at the bottom\n// of the pipe, behind every earlier command) keep the cheap flush.\n// The autotuner is UNCHANGED: the window is what became honest.\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\nenum TimestampSampling {\n    /// As commands of the stream: the begin is written at the bottom of\n    /// the pipe, once every command recorded before it has completed.\n    InCommandStream,\n    /// At the stage boundaries of the pass's encoder (Metal).\n    AtStageBoundaries,\n}\n\nimpl TimestampSampling {\n    fn resolves_after_completion(self) -> bool {\n        matches!(self, TimestampSampling::AtStageBoundaries)\n    }\n\n    fn drains_before_window(self) -> bool {\n        matches!(self, TimestampSampling::AtStageBoundaries)\n    }\n}\n\nfn backend_sampling(is_metal: bool) -> TimestampSampling {\n    if is_metal {\n        TimestampSampling::AtStageBoundaries\n    } else {\n        TimestampSampling::InCommandStream\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Error;\n\nenum PollType {\n    WaitIndefinitely,\n}\n\nstruct Device;\n\nimpl Device {\n    fn poll(&self, _wait: PollType) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        Ok(()) // the wait: work ahead of the window finishes first\n    }\n}\n\nstruct ProfileQuery {\n    sampling: TimestampSampling,\n}\n\nimpl ProfileQuery {\n    fn sampling(&self) -> TimestampSampling {\n        self.sampling\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Profiler {\n    device: Device,\n}\n\nimpl Profiler {\n    fn flush(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    fn start_profile(&mut self, query: &ProfileQuery) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        match query.sampling() {\n            s if s.drains_before_window() => {\n                self.flush()?;\n                // Waits like system timing's sync, for the reason\n                // drains_before_window gives: work still running when\n                // the window's first pass starts is timed as its own.\n                if let Err(err) = self.device.poll(PollType::WaitIndefinitely) {\n                    let _ = format!(\"waiting for the work ahead of a profiled window: {err}\");\n                }\n                Ok(())\n            }\n            _ => self.flush(), // InCommandStream: cannot fail this way\n        }\n    }\n}".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
